## Abstract

We investigate whether a constant hidden-activation intervention can alter locomotion in a frozen reinforcement-learning policy without behavioral cloning or weight updates. Earlier HalfCheetah attempts produced posture changes and slowdowns but did not establish a useful replicated controller. The featured Ant experiment extracts an episode-weighted difference of activation means from natural world-Y velocity variation and selects intervention strength on separate validation episodes. The locked strength {{alpha}} changes world-Y ground-plane velocity by {{effect}} m/s, paired percentile-bootstrap 95% interval {{ci}}, on {{n}} confirmation pairs, retaining {{retention}}% of mean forward speed. Its status remains `{{status}}`: both arms have {{baseline_failures}}/{{n}} failures and a shared pre-onset ending triggers a strict rejection rule. A later audit reproduces {{fresh_effect}} m/s {{fresh_ci}} on {{fresh_n}} further resets, while failures increase from {{fresh_baseline_failures}} to {{fresh_failures}}. Its sign-dependent response ranks at p = {{random_p}} against {{random_n}} isotropic directions; lateral-label specificity is not supported. Projection ablations and an observation intervention support a heading-related explanation, but clipping prevents general physical-heading equivalence. We publish the positive effect, failed quality gates, complete controls, raw episodes, and a saved-data publication workflow. The evidence is specific to the evaluated checkpoint and simulator; cross-training-seed generalization remains untested. [E5, E9; generated values from the accompanying evidence bundle.]
